+### Next
+Dispatch QM with the new watchbill targeting DOM element persistence scenarios.
+
+### Voyage 2 — Acceptance test failures revealed DOM element detachment defect
+
+#### Acceptance test failures from external Cypress suite
+4 failures, all root cause: `render()` replaces `todoList.innerHTML` entirely on every state change, destroying all `<li>` elements and breaking cached DOM references.
+
+**Tests 1-2 (Mark all as completed / Clear complete state):**
+- Creates 3 todos, caches them as `@todos`, checks/unchecks `#toggle-all`
+- `render()` replaces innerHTML, detaching all cached `<li>` references
+- Then `cy.eq()` on the cached reference fails: element not attached to DOM
+
+**Tests 3-4 (Mark/un-mark items as complete):**
+- Creates first todo, caches as `@firstTodo`, creates second todo
+- `render()` from creating second todo replaces innerHTML, detaching `@firstTodo`
+- Then `cy.find('.toggle')` on the cached reference fails
+
+#### App spec violation
+`assets/app-spec.md` Item section says:
+> "Clicking the checkbox marks the todo as complete by updating its `completed` value and toggling the class `completed` on its parent `<li>`"
+
+The phrase "toggling the class `completed` on its parent `<li>`" implies the EXISTING `<li>` element is mutated in place, not replaced with a new one. The current implementation replaces `todoList.innerHTML`, which violates this spec.
+
+#### Durable artifacts authored (this session)
+- `features/todos.feature` — 4 new scenarios under Rule: DOM element persistence
+- `watchbill.json` — 4 scenarios targeted
+- `CAPTAIN.md` — this update
+
+#### New scenarios added
+| # | Scenario | Acceptance test it covers |
+|---|----------|--------------------------|
+| 1 | Existing todo element persists when marked completed | Tests 3 & 4 (find .toggle on cached ref) |
+| 2 | Existing todo elements persist when marking all as completed | Test 1 (eq() on cached @todos after check) |
+| 3 | Clearing the completed state preserves remaining elements | Test 2 (eq() on cached @todos after uncheck) |
+| 4 | Existing todo elements persist when creating a new todo | Tests 3 & 4 (find .toggle on cached ref after 2nd create) |
+
+All 4 scenarios assert that a saved element reference remains attached to the document after a state-changing action — asserting element persistence, not just functional outcome.
+
+### Open
+- QM needs to implement step definitions for "save the element reference", "saved element should have class", "saved element should be attached to the document", and "each saved element should..."
+- The existing 22 scenarios + 1 @contract continue to pass in the in-harness (happy-dom) tier
